\verb{KrylovKitJL(_CG, _GMRES)}, \verb{GinkgoJL(_CG, _GMRES)},
\verb{ConjugateGradientsJL(_CG, _BICGSTAB)} and \code{SimpleGMRES()} name the same
methods (\verb{_IDRS} runs BiCGSTAB, which is IDR(1)). \code{AlgebraicMultigridJL()},
\code{AMGXPreconditioner()}, \code{HYPREAlgorithm()} and \code{PETScAlgorithm()} run
GMRES (R has no algebraic multigrid). \code{InvPreconditioner(P)} applies the
inverse of \code{P}.

\code{LinearSolveFunction(f)} solves with \code{f(A, b, u, p, ...)}. Without an
algorithm, \code{OperatorAssumptions(issquare, condition)} steer the default as
LinearSolve's \code{defaultalg}: square systems use LU unless the condition
is \code{VeryIllConditioned} (QR) or \code{SuperIllConditioned} (SVD); rectangular
ones use QR, or the normal equations by Cholesky when \code{WellConditioned}
(\code{OperatorCondition} lists the levels). \code{init(prob, alg)} returns a
\code{LinearCache} that keeps the factorization: change \code{cache$A} or \code{cache$b}
and call \code{solve_integrator(cache)} again. \code{LowRankUpdatedMatrix(A, U, C, V)} represents \verb{A + U C V'} and is solved with the Woodbury identity.
\code{LinearSolveAdjoint()} is the sensitivity method of linear solves: the
gradient of a loss \code{g(u)} is \verb{-lambda u'} for \code{A} and \code{lambda} for \code{b},
\verb{lambda = A' \\ g_u} (see \code{linear_adjoint()}). \code{LinearVerbosity()} holds
verbosity settings.
